Task: dispatch-refactor-tests (plan agui_hitl_interrupts_a1f3c2d8, phase P2)

Why: P2 restructures the FunctionCall branch in processor.go into a
dispatch table keyed by call name. That is meant to be behaviour
preserving, but the existing tests assert fields one at a time and would
pass through a reordered or thinned stream. The baseline makes 'no
behaviour change' a checkable claim.

Changes:
- agui/interrupt_baseline_test.go (new): canonicalStream helper plus
  TestConfirmationInterruptWireBaseline with two subtests.

Method: render the SSE body to indented canonical JSON, drop only
'timestamp' (wall clock), compare the rest verbatim against an inline
golden. Inline rather than a testdata file because the repo has no
golden/-update harness and the diff is readable in the failure output.

Cases:
- hint plus original call args: covers the hitl.summary block and the
  args passthrough.
- typed ToolConfirmation with payload and no hint: covers
  confirmationPayload and the absence of the hitl block.

Mutation check: deleted confirmationCallName from adkMeta in
processor.go, confirmed the baseline failed, reverted. The pre-existing
field assertions in stream_test.go do not check that key and did not
catch it, which is the gap this test closes.

Findings worth carrying forward:
- A tool call with nil Args emits TOOL_CALL_ARGS delta "null", not
  "{}". Clients calling JSON.parse get null rather than an empty
  object. Pinned as current behaviour, not fixed: the P2 refactor must
  not change the wire, and changing it is a separate protocol decision.
- Only 'timestamp' is volatile in these streams; everything else is
  deterministic, so a full-stream golden is viable for the P3 and P4
  emission and resume work too.

Verification: go fmt, go vet, CI=true go test ./... all clean.
